A||||5||RAGE||Quad 4 H.O.||||5||
Z||000000||RAGE||10-04-2001||07:43 PM||||OK first off I'm new to this site, I just bought an 84 Fiero with the Tech 4, and I am swapping it with a Quad 4 H.O. my question is do I need some sort of kit to do this and if so where could I find one, A.C.E. has one but its $1800, will the engine pretty much bolt up with a new tranny, need as much help as possible PLZ.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Hank is Here||10-04-2001||08:02 PM||||Rage, <BR>Welcome.  The Q4 swap is not the easiest from what I have read.  You need to get the tranny wih the engine since it is a different bolt pattern.  Also I beleive you want the 91-93 ish Q4 engine that has the most HP and did not bolw head gaskets as much.   If you look on the upper right you can use the search function and read some of the old posts about it here.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||10-04-2001||08:05 PM||||I suspect that the quad 4 is not a direct swap without some re engineering thus ACE sells a kit. There are some wiring modifications that may be required, engine mounts etc.  Use the search function on this site for historical data on quad 4 conversions.<P>Its just like a V8 isn't a direct swap, so you get an installation kit from Archie.||||reg||3
Z||000003||Standard||10-04-2001||10:08 PM||||I wouldn't do it with an '84.. the wiring differences will make it a pain.  I'm not sure about other years, but I know the Quad bolts up to the stock mounts on an 88 v6 Fiero.  You may have to weld a mount on the '84.  <P>You <B>will need</B> a tranny that came on a quad or 2.4l twin cam.  They have a unique bellhousing pattern.<P>This is not an easy engine swap.  Especially if this is your first one.  If you're going to be doing a lot of city driving, you might want to reconsider your engine.  A 3800 series II puts out 205hp, 230(?) ft/lb torque.  The drawbacks are more weight and less fuel economy, but it will be much easier to drive in the city.  You have to take the quad above 3k rpm  to get any power, and 4k is where it really starts kicking in.  It does sound damn good winding up through 6k, though.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif"> <P>oh ya.. about the wiring.  You have to splice the Fiero and quad wiring harness's together.  You ditch the stock Fiero computer, and use the quad to drive the gauges and everything.  <P>Ask Pherder, he's done two  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  I'm driving one of them.  <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">          <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||pherder||10-04-2001||11:05 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Standard:<BR><B>I wouldn't do it with an '84.. the wiring differences will make it a pain.  I'm not sure about other years, but I know the Quad bolts up to the stock mounts on an 88 v6 Fiero.  You may have to weld a mount on the '84.  <P>You [b]will need</B> a tranny that came on a quad or 2.4l twin cam.  They have a unique bellhousing pattern.<P>This is not an easy engine swap.  Especially if this is your first one.  If you're going to be doing a lot of city driving, you might want to reconsider your engine.  A 3800 series II puts out 205hp, 230(?) ft/lb torque.  The drawbacks are more weight and less fuel economy, but it will be much easier to drive in the city.  You have to take the quad above 3k rpm  to get any power, and 4k is where it really starts kicking in.  It does sound damn good winding up through 6k, though.   <P>oh ya.. about the wiring.  You have to splice the Fiero and quad wiring harness's together.  You ditch the stock Fiero computer, and use the quad to drive the gauges and everything.  <P>Ask Pherder, he's done two    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  I'm driving one of them.  <P>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Well, I guess I should correct some of the mis-understandings here ...<P>First, the Quad 4 (or any computer controlled engine) that wasn't originally installed into the Fiero will be a BIG job to make it fit/work.<P>The Q4 (as already posted) REQUIRES you to use a Q4 transaxle or a TwinCam transaxle.  The TwinCam is an updated Q4 available 1996-?<P>The best/easiest found Q4 engine is the 92 HO Quad 4 (Olds, Chevy, and Pontiac had them) and is rated @ ~ 180 HP.<P>The HIGHEST HP is the 92-93 W-41 Quad 4 found ONLY in the Olds Achieva SCX ... rated @ ~195 HP.<P>No matter the year Fiero (84-88), modifications to the wiring harness will be required ... doesn't matter what you start with, you end up with the Q4 computer and wiring harness mated to the Fiero's rear harness.<P>A front engine bracket will have to be custom made.  The one I had made fit the 88 cradle (Standard ... the 88 Coupe you have is a 4 cylinder car!!  No differences in the cradle from 4 to 6 with the manual transaxle!)  If using a TwinCam (Isuzu) 5 speed, it requires a custom front transaxle bracket.  The older Q4's Getrag transaxle in some cases will use the Fiero's Getrag brackets but NOT all!  Some of the Q4 Getrags have a slave that bolts to the bell housing and pushed the clutch forks internally (Rodney Dickman now makes an adapter to make the Getrag slave work on this kind of transaxle ... the newer Isuzu is the same way ... i.e. the transaxle that bolts to the TwinCam engines).  Some Q4 Getrags have a hydraulic throwout bearing.  To make it work, the clutch MASTER cylinder will have to be replaced with a larger, aftermarket version (i.e. custom mounted.)<P>Be advised, the HO Q4s ONLY came with a 5 speed Getrag transaxle.  If you find a Q4 with an automatic (3T40/T125C), it is the lower power version (150-160 HP).<P><B>ALL</B> Quad 4s suffer from head gasket problems.  The factory has strengthen the head gaskets but the problem is a coolant hole in the head ~ 1/4" from the combustion chamber!  With an aluminum head and a cast iron block ... they move around a little normally during heat cycles. <P>Let's see, have I forgotten anything ... custom coolant hookups, custom air cleaner setup, custom dog bone, custom exhaust system (it is all in the rear of the car!), custom fuel lines, etc. ???<P>As I started ... any engine, not originally installed in the Fiero will have a lot of work to make it right ... some are just easier than others.  The ones that use the stock Fiero's transaxle are easier to a point.<P>As Standard pointed out, I have done two of them ... one a 160 HP AUTOMATIC Quad 4.  It was a <B>DOG</B> under 3000 rpms and screamed aboved that.  Driving around town wasn't much fun ... I got rid of it.<P>Built the HO Q4 with an Isuzu 5 speed because the original Getrag 5 speed had been sold by the salvage yard and the TwinCam transaxle did have the right bolt pattern.  I though it had the 3.88:1 FDR in it ... it didn't ... it had the 3.35:1 FDR so the car will do 70 @ 2400 rpm in 5th!  Not the best gearing for "racing" but great for gas mileage (Standard ... didn't you get 38 mpg on the highway on your return trip home?)<P>I run out of energy ...<P>Paul<BR>------------------<BR>Paul Herder<BR>88 Formula - Artero kit car doner and 350 TPI<BR>86 SE - 3.2 w/440T4 Transaxle<BR>95 GMC - 350 TBI - Factory "Custom"<BR>92 Mazda Protege - Stock<BR>Sedgwick, KS 67135<p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||13
Z||000005||Philphine||10-05-2001||02:11 AM||||if you can find a "big fiero parts book" there was a company in there that had a kit for $900. i think it was supposed to have everything but the engine. can't find my book but if you search the achives i remember telling someone else the same thing including the company name. ||||reg||1
